WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.875 --> 00:00:03.023
There are two ways to plan a project.

2
00:00:03.752 --> 00:00:06.724
The first is called waterfall: phases in order.

3
00:00:07.446 --> 00:00:10.012
Analyse, design, build, implement.

4
00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:14.332
Each phase is signed off before the next one starts.

5
00:00:15.064 --> 00:00:19.441
And the scope is fixed: what the project covers is on paper up front.

6
00:00:20.510 --> 00:00:23.993
You plan once in detail, and manage deviations.

7
00:00:24.709 --> 00:00:28.227
That fits work that is predictable and hard to change later:

8
00:00:28.401 --> 00:00:32.047
installing a new medium-voltage substation in a neighbourhood,

9
00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:33.858
or moving a production line.

10
00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:38.321
The second way is agile: working in short rounds.

11
00:00:39.056 --> 00:00:43.502
Each round ends with something working, which users see and react to.

12
00:00:44.506 --> 00:00:46.178
The scope adjusts as you learn.

13
00:00:46.905 --> 00:00:49.401
What the planners say steers the next round.

14
00:00:50.459 --> 00:00:53.048
The term comes from the Agile Manifesto,

15
00:00:53.176 --> 00:00:57.112
written by seventeen software developers in 2001.

16
00:00:58.113 --> 00:01:02.478
It fits work where the solution is unclear and the users' reaction matters:

17
00:01:02.594 --> 00:01:03.767
a new planning meeting,

18
00:01:03.906 --> 00:01:06.971
or a new dashboard of key figures for shift leads.

19
00:01:08.314 --> 00:01:10.532
The best-known agile method is Scrum.

20
00:01:11.273 --> 00:01:16.648
Its rounds are called sprints: a fixed period of at most a month, often two weeks.

21
00:01:17.662 --> 00:01:21.331
Each sprint starts with a short planning, and ends with a review,

22
00:01:21.481 --> 00:01:23.048
where users see the result.

23
00:01:24.148 --> 00:01:27.759
Then the team looks back at how it worked: the retrospective.

24
00:01:28.823 --> 00:01:31.377
In a factory you rarely need the full method.

25
00:01:32.127 --> 00:01:36.226
The core is enough: short rounds, something real at the end of each,

26
00:01:36.423 --> 00:01:37.979
and feedback from the floor.

27
00:01:39.307 --> 00:01:42.720
Most improvement projects are hybrid: fixed milestones,

28
00:01:42.918 --> 00:01:47.109
budget and end date for the steering group, and short rounds inside.

29
00:01:48.144 --> 00:01:51.836
The steering group sees one date: pilot live in week 10.

30
00:01:52.547 --> 00:01:55.287
The team works towards it in two-week rounds.

31
00:01:56.446 --> 00:02:01.739
A pilot is a small, bounded trial in one area, with an end date and a way back.

32
00:02:02.787 --> 00:02:05.434
Experts argue whether pilots prove much:

33
00:02:05.562 --> 00:02:09.648
a pilot with extra attention often does better than the rollout will.

34
00:02:10.976 --> 00:02:11.882
An example.

35
00:02:12.614 --> 00:02:16.829
At a grid operator you lead a project to introduce a readiness check:

36
00:02:17.003 --> 00:02:18.175
a check that permits,

37
00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:22.425
materials and crew are in place before a job enters the week plan.

38
00:02:23.484 --> 00:02:26.363
Nobody knows exactly which checks will matter.

39
00:02:27.109 --> 00:02:30.859
As a waterfall, the planners would first see the check at the go-live.

40
00:02:31.573 --> 00:02:33.001
So you work in rounds.

41
00:02:34.145 --> 00:02:38.244
For the steering group, a milestone plan: baseline done in week 4,

42
00:02:38.348 --> 00:02:42.980
pilot live in week 8, and the go/no-go for rollout in week 16.

43
00:02:44.029 --> 00:02:48.499
Inside it, the team works in two-week rounds, with the planners involved.

44
00:02:49.973 --> 00:02:54.548
Round one: a paper checklist with five checks, used by two planners.

45
00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:59.608
At the review they say: check four takes too long, and never fails.

46
00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:01.169
So it goes.

47
00:03:02.261 --> 00:03:06.522
Round two: add the switching plan check the control room asked for.

48
00:03:07.542 --> 00:03:11.408
By week 8 the checklist has been tested on 60 real jobs.

49
00:03:12.156 --> 00:03:15.036
And the planners helped shape it, so they defend it.

50
00:03:16.421 --> 00:03:18.801
Then the people side: don't sell jargon.

51
00:03:19.537 --> 00:03:23.508
A plant manager who hears Scrum may think of software and chaos.

52
00:03:24.501 --> 00:03:27.241
Say instead: we work in two-week rounds,

53
00:03:27.438 --> 00:03:30.642
and every round you'll see something working on the floor.

54
00:03:31.654 --> 00:03:32.455
Resistance?

55
00:03:33.166 --> 00:03:34.803
The board wants a full plan.

56
00:03:35.614 --> 00:03:39.527
Then give them the milestone plan; the rounds are how the team gets there.

57
00:03:40.599 --> 00:03:42.561
And if you're unsure which approach fits,

58
00:03:42.771 --> 00:03:45.848
ask: how sure are we about what the solution looks like?
